# Windows clones default to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites every checked-out
# file to CRLF. A shell script with CRLF line endings is not portable: Git for
# Windows' own bash tolerates the stray \r, but a bash reached any other way
# (WSL, a Linux container mounting the same checkout, an msys build without the
# igncr patch) fails on the first line with a bare `$'\r': command not found`.
# Pin the endings per file type so the checkout is correct whatever the clone's
# autocrlf happens to be.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.md    text
